--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b8cb648fdf2cd024d97f20a78acfcd4c, type: 3}
  m_Name: PracticalGuide_ScrewGauge_Azm
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::VirtualLab.Azm.Core.PracticalGuide_Azm
  title: 'Screw Gauge: Thickness of a Metal Strip (Exp 1.3)'
  aim: Measure the thickness of a metal strip with a screw gauge.
  description: 'A screw gauge measures lengths as small as a hundredth of a millimetre.
    It has two scales: the main scale on the sleeve and the circular scale on the
    thimble. Turning the thimble one full turn moves the spindle by one pitch.'
  apparatus:
  - Screw gauge
  - Flat metal strip (or a kink-free wire)
  whatYouMeasure: The pitch and least count of the screw gauge, its zero error, then
    the thickness of the strip three times at different places. The result is the
    mean of the three corrected readings.
  procedure:
  - "Find the least count: LC = pitch \xF7 number of divisions on the circular scale."
  - Close the anvil and spindle by turning the ratchet until it clicks.
  - Check the zero error and work out the zero correction.
  - Hold the metal strip between the anvil and the spindle.
  - Tighten gently with the ratchet until it clicks.
  - 'Note the main-scale reading M: the mark just under or before the edge of the
    thimble.'
  - Note the division n of the circular scale that lies on the datum line.
  - "Fraction X = n \xD7 LC; observed thickness Y = M + X."
  - "Apply the zero correction: t = Y \xB1 ZC."
  - Take two more readings at different places and find the mean thickness.
  formulas:
  - Pitch = distance moved on the main scale for one full turn = 0.5 mm
  - "Least count LC = pitch \xF7 number of circular divisions = 0.5 mm \xF7 50 =
    0.01 mm"
  - "Zero error: circular 0 below the datum line = +n \xD7 LC; above it = \u2212(50
    \u2212 n) \xD7 LC"
  - "Zero correction ZC = \u2212(zero error)"
  - "X = n \xD7 LC;  Y = M + X;  t = Y + ZC;  mean t = (t\u2081 + t\u2082 + t\u2083)
    / 3"
  precautions:
  - text: Add or subtract the zero correction as required.
    why: A zero error shifts every reading by the same amount.
  - text: Take readings at different places.
    why: A strip or wire is not exactly the same thickness everywhere; the mean is
      more reliable.
  - text: Do not press the jaws very hard.
    why: Squeezing bends the strip and the frame, so the reading comes out too small.
  - text: Do not turn the thimble back and forth unnecessarily.
    why: It wears the screw, which spoils the pitch.
  - text: Turn the screw gently using the ratchet.
    why: 'The ratchet slips once the spindle touches, so every reading uses the same
      light pressure. (Think: this is what the ratchet is for.)'
  - text: Use a flat strip (or a kink-free wire).
    why: 'A bend or kink holds the spindle away from the metal, so the reading is
      too large. (Think: why it must be flat.)'
  observationTable: "For readings 1\u20133: main-scale reading M (mm), circular division
    n on the datum line, fraction X = n \xD7 LC (mm), observed thickness Y = M +
    X (mm), corrected thickness t = Y \xB1 ZC (mm). LC, zero error and zero correction
    are written above the table; mean t = (t\u2081 + t\u2082 + t\u2083)/3."
